There's variance by field in the U.S.  In some, a short or extensive
postdoc is scommon (seems to be predominantly in lab sciences).

"Associate Professor" and "Ph.D. Candidate" would nto be found in the
U.S. outside of some very bizarre situations.  While uncommon, there are
some schools that while hire ABD's (All But Dissertatioin) as assistant
professors (Lecturers in UKese), but the norm is to hire them into the
spot as an "Instructor" with an automatic promotion to assistant
professor when completing the degree--and even this is the exception.

The bizarre situations come from the general requirement for a
tenure-line hire:  "terminal degree" in the field.  For most fields,
this is a Ph.D.  In some, it's a master's--the associate professor in
English two doors down has an MFA (Master in Fine Arts), which is
apparently the terminal degree for poets.  If a field changed, and the
Ph.D. became normal/available, such a person could conceivably get the
degree, but generally there's no reason to do so.

Also, there's a handful of assistant and associate professors, most near
retirement age, that only have their masters or bachelors.  It wasn't
rare to hire as such in the 60's (my father was offered one with his BA,
but would have been expected to get a higher degree in the field, but he
chose to stay with high school).  Often the higher degree or serious
scholarly work was required for promotion to associate professor, and
maybe tenure.  Some schools (such as this one) would tenure assistant
professors based solely on teaching (though research would be required
if they ever wanted promotion).

